%[text] # Exercise 3 — Assess a Characterized Ground Radar
%[text] This live script runs a wide-area surveillance baseline with three stationary ground radars and two airborne radars. The code below is based on [this documentation example](https://www.mathworks.com/help/fusion/ug/wide-area-surveillance-using-radars-example.html), reformatted to expose the radar object at the top level.
%[text] ![](text:image:5100)
%%
%[text] ## Setup
exercise3Paths = setupExercise3();
%%
%[text] ## Build the Original Ground-Radar Model
%[text] Configure Radar Parameters
updateRate = 1;
elevationAccuracy = atan2d(0.9, 463);
elevationBiasFraction = 0.05;
%[text] Configure Radar Object
groundRadar = radarDataGenerator(1, ... %[output:group:48043e93] %[output:2543316f]
    UpdateRate=updateRate, ... %[output:2543316f]
    FieldOfView=[30; 20], ... %[output:2543316f]
    HasElevation=true, ... %[output:2543316f]
    ScanMode="Mechanical", ... %[output:2543316f]
    MechanicalAzimuthLimits=[0 360], ... %[output:2543316f]
    MechanicalElevationLimits=[-20+1e-3 0], ... %[output:2543316f]
    DetectionProbability=0.9, ... %[output:2543316f]
    HasINS=true, ... %[output:2543316f]
    HasRangeRate=true, ... %[output:2543316f]
    HasNoise=true, ... %[output:2543316f]
    HasFalseAlarms=true, ... %[output:2543316f]
    ReferenceRange=200e3, ... %[output:2543316f]
    ReferenceRCS=10, ... %[output:2543316f]
    RangeLimits=[0 463000], ... %[output:2543316f]
    AzimuthResolution=2.8, ... %[output:2543316f]
    AzimuthBiasFraction=0.176/2.8, ... %[output:2543316f]
    ElevationResolution=elevationAccuracy/elevationBiasFraction, ... %[output:2543316f]
    ElevationBiasFraction=elevationBiasFraction, ... %[output:2543316f]
    RangeResolution=323, ... %[output:2543316f]
    RangeBiasFraction=116/323, ... %[output:2543316f]
    RangeRateResolution=100, ... %[output:2543316f]
    FalseAlarmRate=1e-7, ... %[output:2543316f]
    RangeRateLimits=[-500 500], ... %[output:2543316f]
    DetectionCoordinates="Sensor spherical") %[output:group:48043e93] %[output:2543316f]
%%
%[text] ## Build and Run the Original Scenario
%[text] Similar to the original documentation example, a helper function is used to generate the scenario.  You can open that helper function to inspect that code. Agentic AI can be prompted to explain the helper functions in this example.
scenario = helperCreateWideAreaSurveillanceScenario(groundRadar);
%[text] To simplify the example for the workshop, we put the tracking simulation loop inside a helper function as well, again this can be inspected manually or by prompting an agent.
baselineResults = helperRunWideAreaSurveillanceTracking(scenario, groundRadar); %[output:6116da69]
%%
%[text] ## Baseline OSPA History
%[text] The helper above runs the scenario, but also collects the Optimal Sub Pattern Assignment (OSPA) metric using the [`trackOSPAMetric`](https://www.mathworks.com/help/fusion/ref/trackospametric-system-object.html) class.  We can use this as a single cost value per time step to quantify the tracking accuracy for the whole scenario.  There are other more granular metrics available in the toolbox as well, such as [`trackErrorMetrics`](https://www.mathworks.com/help/fusion/ref/trackerrormetrics-system-object.html) and [`trackAssignmentMetrics`](https://www.mathworks.com/help/fusion/ref/trackassignmentmetrics-system-object.html)`.`
plot(baselineResults.Time, baselineResults.OSPA, LineWidth=1.5)
grid on
xlabel("Scenario time (s)")
ylabel("OSPA")
title("Baseline Wide-Area Tracking Performance")
meanBaselineOSPA = mean(baselineResults.OSPA)

